            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Alexandre Dumas's Background</strong><br>\nAlexandre Dumas was born in 1802 in the village of Villers-Cotterêts, France. His father, a general in Napoleon's army, died when Dumas was four years old, leaving the family in poverty. Despite his modest upbringing, Dumas pursued literature with voracious ambition.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Three Musketeers</strong><br>\nIn 1844, Dumas published \"The Three Musketeers,\" a novel set in the 17th century that follows the adventures of a young man named d'Artagnan after he leaves home to travel to Paris, seeking to join the Musketeers of the Guard. The book became a huge success.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Count of Monte Cristo</strong><br>\nThe Count of Monte Cristo, published in 1844-1845, is another of Dumas's most popular works. This novel tells the story of Edmond Dantès, a man wrongfully imprisoned, who escapes and uses a hidden treasure to exact his revenge on those who betrayed him.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
